--- ⛔ THE EXIT 1: A DEFECTIVE POST-COMMIT GUARD, NOT A FAILED RESTORE ---------
##[error]restore-test-from-baseline: post-census extensions=7, pre-census had 6. An extension that lived in public was CASCADE-dropped and the dump did not put it back.
##[error]Process completed with exit code 1.

  WHAT FIRED: `scripts/restore-test-from-baseline.sh:1090`, an equality check on
  a WHOLE-DATABASE `SELECT count(*) FROM pg_extension` (`:282`).

  WHY IT IS DEFECTIVE, three ways, all measured:
    1. DIRECTION. It fired on a GAIN (6 -> 7) and reported it in the language of
       a LOSS ("was CASCADE-dropped and the dump did not put it back"). A reader
       of that message is sent to look for a missing extension.
    2. SCOPE. Its comment reasons about "an extension that lived IN public", but
       the count it compares is every row of pg_extension in the database, so an
       extension created in `extensions` or `pg_catalog` moves the number.
    3. ORDERING. It runs AFTER the COMMIT, so it cannot prevent anything; it can
       only mislabel a committed result. The five shape assertions ahead of it
       (tables / policies / functions / ledger_rows / survivors, `:1073-1077`)
       had ALL already passed — the guard that fired is the first failing one.

  WHICH EXTENSION, IDENTIFIED BY DIFFING TWO FILES IN THIS RUN'S OWN BACKUP
  ARTIFACT (test-restore-backup-34274355596), not by inference:
    schema-before.sql (TEST as it was) CREATE EXTENSION set — 5 named:
